The `engines:` half of `hub/internal/resumerecipes/recipes.yaml` transcribes
the native-resume recipe table from that project's `src/agent_resume.rs`
(fn `plan`) at commit 6f311498aeeb27c0973781961ef94e8d0016ed17 (v0.8.0) —
16 engine CLIs, each with the flag or subcommand that reattaches to a prior
session — together with the session-reference validation envelope
(`MAX_SESSION_ID_LEN` 512, `MAX_SESSION_PATH_LEN` 4096, control-character
screening, absolute-path requirement) and the restore-time dedupe key.

The transcription is data, not code: the Go evaluator, the shell-quoting
layer, the family mapping and the per-row provenance and verification grades
are ours. The `gemini` row is ours as well and has no upstream counterpart.
`hub/internal/resumerecipes/recipes_test.go` pins each transcribed row to the
upstream argv so a re-vendor can be diffed.

`hub/internal/panestate/manifests/vendor/` is a verbatim copy of that
project's `src/detect/manifests/` at the same commit — 19 TOML files carrying
the per-agent screen-state detection rules. The directory is unmodified, so it
can be diffed against upstream directly; each file is pinned by git blob SHA in
`hub/internal/panestate/panestate_test.go`, and everything of ours lives in
`manifests/overlay/`. The rule schema, the validation caps, the region
vocabulary and the strict-blocked fallback semantics are ported from
`src/detect/manifest.rs`; the Go evaluator, the Rust-regex-to-RE2 dialect
translation and the agent-family mapping are ours. The screen corpus in
`hub/internal/panestate/testdata/screen_corpus.json` is lifted from that
project's own tests, screens and expected answers together.
